    "result_text": "The full suite passes: **283 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field on the owner-creation response. When an owner is created, `locality` is `'local'` if their city is the single most common city among the owners that already exist at that moment, otherwise `'remote'`.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `locality` string to the `Owner` schema (regenerates `OwnerDto`).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 Added the `locality` field (`@Column(name = \"locality\")`) with getter/setter, so MapStruct maps it automatically by name.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added `owner.setLocality(resolveLocality(owner.getCity()))` in `addOwner`, plus a `resolveLocality` helper that:\n   - Builds a case-insensitive frequency map of existing owners' cities (computed *before* saving the new owner).\n   - Returns `'local'` only when exactly one city holds the maximum count *and* the new owner's city is that city; otherwise `'remote'`.\n   - Returns `'remote'` when there are no existing owners, a null city, or a tie for most common (no *single* most common city).\n\n4. **`schema.sql`** (h2, hsqldb, postgres, mysql) \u2014 Added a `locality` column to the `owners` table so the value persists.\n\n5. **Tests** \u2014 Extended `createOwnerSuccess` to assert `locality == \"local\"` for a Madison owner (Madison is the single most common seeded city, 4 owners vs. Monona's 2), and added `createOwnerRemoteLocalityForUncommonCity` asserting `remote` for an uncommon city.\n\nThe logic mirrors the existing computed-field pattern (`customerCode`, `namesakeCount`, `sharesHousehold`) already established in the controller.",
